comment:
Major ESR-line rebase, 140.14.0esr -> 153.2.0esr (not a point release), plus the
toolchain bump needed to build it. Reviewed the spec, the .changes and the patch
churn; the actual CVE fixes come from the upstream tarball and were not audited
individually (security-bug policy).

Spec changes, all consistent with the rebase:
  - %major 140 -> 153, %mainver %major.2.0, %orig_version 153.2.0.
  - new %gcc_version macro: gcc13 for sle_version <= 150000, gcc15 otherwise, so
    15-SP7 (150700) now builds with gcc15; CC/CXX and additionally AR/NM/RANLIB are
    pointed at the versioned toolchain (needed for LTO correctness).
  - build-time dependency bumps: cargo/rust 1.86 -> 1.94, rust-cbindgen >= 0.29.4,
    clang15 -> clang19, python39 -> python311, mozilla-nspr-devel >= 4.39,
    mozilla-nss-devel >= 3.125, new libproxy-devel / libstdc++6-devel-gccN / glibc-devel.
    These are build-time only and the package built for all four arches.
  - new mozconfig option --enable-libproxy.
  - LTO condition tightened from suse_version > 1600 to >= 1699; 15-SP7 is unaffected
    (LTO was already off for it).

Patches added (4): fix-missing-libfreebl3-symbols.patch (link -lfreebl3 against the
system NSS), fix-skia-musttail.patch (excludes s390x from SKCMS_HAS_MUSTTAIL, i.e. an
s390x build fix), thunderbird-silence-no-return.patch (-Wreturn-type fix), and
mozilla-sandbox-lto.patch.

=> HAS_UNTRACKED_CHANGES is YES for exactly one reason: mozilla-sandbox-lto.patch is
added to the spec as Patch25 but is NOT mentioned anywhere in the .changes, while the
other three are covered by "Added patches to fix build: ...". The patch itself is
harmless (it makes --param lto-partitions=1 unconditional for non-clang builds in
security/sandbox/linux/moz.build, a gcc-LTO build workaround), so this is a changelog
hygiene defect rather than a functional risk, but the packager should add an entry.

Patches removed (5), each justified in the .changes:
  - mozilla-kde-force-xdg-portal.patch and mozilla-nongnome-proxies.patch: removed as
    Leap-only/KDE-only; SLE ships no KDE, and proxy handling is now covered by
    --enable-libproxy. Worth a note: this does change how proxy settings are resolved
    on SLE desktops, though libproxy is the correct mechanism.
  - mozilla-bmo998749.patch and bmo2006630-remove-gitmodules-from-cargo-checksum-files.patch:
    upstreamed.
  - thunderbird-fix-CVE-2024-34703.patch: I checked this one specifically, because a
    patch named after a CVE being deleted while that CVE is still listed in the
    changelog is exactly the shape of a silently reintroduced vulnerability. It is
    safe: the .changes groups it with the upstreamed patches --
        "- Removed upstreamed patch mozilla-bmo998749.patch,
           bmo2006630-remove-gitmodules-from-cargo-checksum-files.patch,
           thunderbird-fix-CVE-2024-34703.patch"
    (source.diff lines 951-953). It was a downstream backport of Botan upstream commit
    94e9154c14 into the 140.x vendored Botan copy, and is redundant against the Botan
    version vendored in 153.x. CVE-2024-34703 correctly remains tracked in _patchinfo.
    Caveat stated honestly: I relied on the changelog's "upstreamed" classification and
    did not re-verify the fix inside the 153.2.0 tarball, since per-CVE source
    archaeology is out of scope for security bugs.

All three tracked bugs are documented in the .changes, each against its MFSA:
bsc#1271649 / MFSA 2026-71, bsc#1274867 / MFSA 2026-80, bsc#1278001 / MFSA 2026-88.

